      "id": 3,
      "claim": "\"Deadliest revolution\" is conventionally defined as the European revolution with the highest total number of deaths directly attributable to the revolutionary period (combat, executions, civilian killings, and immediate aftermath).",
      "evidence": "Comparative studies of revolutions (e.g., Goldstone 2001, Collier 2009) use total mortality as the primary metric for deadliness.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "The French Revolution (1789‑1799) caused between 40 000 and 100 000 deaths.",
      "evidence": "Scholars such as Georges Lefebvre and Simon Schama estimate the death toll from the Reign of Terror, wars, and related violence in that range.",
      "weight": 0.10,
      "depends_on": [3]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "The Russian Revolution of 1917 and the ensuing civil war resulted in roughly 7 million to 12 million deaths.",
      "evidence": "Demographic research by Orlando Figes, Robert Service, and others documents millions of combat, famine, and repression deaths linked to the revolutionary period.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [3]
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "claim": "Other major European revolutions (e.g., the Spanish Civil War 1936‑1939, Hungarian uprising 1956) produced death tolls ranging from tens of thousands to several hundred thousand.",
